<p>British MPs voted 335 to 223 on Tuesday to reject a Conservative motion to refer Prime Minister Keir Starmer to the House of Commons Privileges Committee over statements he made about Peter Mandelson’s appointment as UK ambassador to the United States. Conservative leader Kemi Badenoch sought an inquiry into whether Starmer misled Parliament by saying “full due process” had been followed and that no pressure existed in relation to the appointment. The vote followed testimony from Starmer’s former chief of staff, Morgan McSweeney, who said his advice on the appointment was a “serious error of judgment” but denied asking officials to ignore procedures or clear checks “at all costs”. Former Foreign Office chief Sir Philip Barton declined to endorse Starmer’s claim that full due process had been followed, saying the appointment had been announced before security vetting was completed and that Starmer knew of risks linked to Mandelson’s association with Jeffrey Epstein. Starmer appointed Mandelson in December 2024 and dismissed him in September 2025 after information showed his ties to Epstein were deeper than previously known, according to Reuters-based reports. Starmer has said Mandelson lied about the relationship and that officials withheld vetting information that would have prevented him from making the appointment.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/DrI2x9RJ6tYwW2EhibY3swkoKuD5TcyGMUQ4kIHbl1OYo8CdxoG8NclX9QqAm25HGfhdD-kf40CpTgSCqgFYrMTCXot_IJNMFzQdLy4l0DzopP18h5v8ZW5AuBxxumsAB_AjuYwdDs-6pSdinVllNmHh2dBxVMYlE0tqdLQ5vE_ODlvQMSbuyz8dLwAU1Eixc9EP15eaHouO0Q6vedvPnMlDlghRRDgPXNi1OsYb3C8Q6qEfSyrs2ECDoIyVuEsKKyYb4iVXot7fvJ0_Ag' alt='Former No. 10 chief of staff Morgan McSweeney appears before the Foreign Affairs Committee about Lord Peter Mandelson&#x27;s vetting process at the Houses of Parliament in London on April 28, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Labour rebellion: Labour ordered its MPs to oppose the motion, but 15 Labour MPs backed it and 53 did not vote, according to HuffPost UK.</li><li>Vetting concerns: UK Security Vetting had recommended Mandelson not receive clearance before Sir Olly Robbins, the Foreign Office’s top civil servant, cleared him for developed vetting.</li><li>Backbench pressure: Some Labour MPs criticised the whip and suggested Starmer should refer himself to the Privileges Committee to “clear his name”.</li><li>Committee stakes: A Privileges Committee finding that a prime minister knowingly misled Parliament would be expected to make that leader’s position untenable.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Downing Street: A spokesman said the government was “delivering for Britain” and accused the Conservatives of using the vote as a political stunt before local elections. (<a href='https://www.irishnews.com/news/uk/mps-reject-bid-to-investigate-claim-pm-misled-commons-over-mandelson-appointment-Z5V2YL5UTNJNZGITVB2UELV2PQ/'>The Irish News</a>)</li><li>Kemi Badenoch: The Conservative leader said Labour MPs would “rue the day” they voted against the motion and accused them of being “complicit” in a “cover-up.” (<a href='https://www.irishnews.com/news/uk/mps-reject-bid-to-investigate-claim-pm-misled-commons-over-mandelson-appointment-Z5V2YL5UTNJNZGITVB2UELV2PQ/'>The Irish News</a>)</li><li>Morgan McSweeney: Starmer’s former top adviser stopped short of taking full responsibility for the appointment, telling MPs it was ultimately “the Prime Minister’s decision.” (<a href='https://www.irishnews.com/news/uk/mcsweeney-denies-swearing-at-foreign-office-boss-to-speed-up-mandelson-vetting-TP7CZYYKEJNHTL25FED4JCKXVU/'>The Irish News</a>)</li><li>Sir Philip Barton: The former Foreign Office permanent undersecretary said officials showed an “uninterested” attitude toward securing high-level security clearance for Mandelson. (<a href='https://www.irishnews.com/news/uk/starmers-due-process-claim-over-mandelson-job-not-backed-by-ex-official-ZZUO5RCOWFJS3MSA26FJWKV7PQ/'>The Irish News</a>)</li><li>Liberal